  "Five villages where a cold stream, warm river and autumn mist turn rotten grapes into gold.",
  """The Sauternais is a small area about 40 km south-east of Bordeaux, where the Ciron stream joins the Garonne. Its five communes (Sauternes, Bommes, Fargues, Preignac and Barsac) make lusciously sweet white wine from grapes attacked by noble rot, *Botrytis cinerea*. Barsac, on the north side of the Ciron, has its own appellation but may also use the Sauternes name. Across the Garonne, Loupiac, Cadillac and Sainte-Croix-du-Mont make similar wines from clay-limestone slopes.""",
  """Sweet wine made sense here because Dutch merchants wanted it in the seventeenth century, and because the Garonne carried it cheaply to the sea. Noble rot probably came into deliberate use in the late seventeenth or eighteenth century; by the time Thomas Jefferson ordered Yquem in 1787 the style was famous. The 1855 classification gave Sauternes its own list, with Yquem alone as Premier Cru Supérieur. The later twentieth century brought heroic vintages (1921, 1929, 1937, 1945, 1967, 1975, 1988 to 1990, 2001) but also falling demand, and many estates now make dry white wines as well.""",
  """The Ciron rises from springs in the Landes forest and runs under a shady canopy of trees, so its water stays cold. In September and October it meets the warmer Garonne, and on clear nights the temperature difference makes dense morning fog. Fog lets the spores of botrytis germinate on ripe grapes; afternoon sunshine then dries the berries, so the fungus feeds without turning into grey rot. The fungus pierces the skins, water evaporates, and sugar, acidity and glycerol concentrate while the fungus adds its own aromatic compounds. Gravel and sand over clay and limestone dominate Sauternes; Barsac has thin red sand and clay over a limestone plateau.""",
  "Sémillon, with its thin skin and rich texture, is about 80% of plantings; Sauvignon Blanc adds acidity and aroma, and a little Muscadelle brings florals.",
